# Three-pillar research standard

**Doc link:** <https://github.com/brando90/judges-dont-need-psychometrics-or-corr/blob/main/.cursor/rules/ml-research-standards.mdc>

**TLDR:** A claim reaches the paper only if (1) its theorem compiles in Lean 4 with zero `sorry`, (2) a deterministic pytest check agrees on random instances, and (3) its empirical support is a pre-registered, leak-free run on real multi-annotator ratings scored with an unbiased per-sample proper risk and item-clustered intervals.

Brando, 11-27-2026. This restates, in one place, the section "Three-pillar research standard" in `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` (those two files are byte-identical) and ties together `proofs-and-real-data-conference-standard.mdc`, `lean-and-numerics-check.mdc` and `result-clarity.mdc`.

1. **Lean 4.** The theorem is stated in general form and compiled with the pinned toolchain. Zero `sorry`, zero custom axioms; `#print axioms` shows only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`; the compiler output is saved next to the file. If a file imports Mathlib, fetch the prebuilt cache with `lake exe cache get`; never rebuild Mathlib from scratch. State exactly what was machine-checked: for example both the squared form of anti-properness (`rho^2 < rho`) and the square-root form (`sqrt(rho) > rho`, `PlanAudit.Model.anti_proper` in experiment 35's `PlanClaims.lean`) are Lean-checked, for a finite joint law. See also `every-proof-in-lean.mdc`. A claim argued by hand is labelled "analytical", never "proved" or "verified".
2. **pytest numerical checks.** Every theorem has a deterministic test (fixed seed; exact fractions where the quantity is rational, a named tolerance otherwise) that runs under `pytest`, tests every claimed-false statement as a failing case, and gives identical output on a second run. A failing check is resolved by deciding whether the claim or the check is wrong, and saying which.
3. **Pre-registered, leak-free real-data experiments.** `PROTOCOL.md` (hypotheses, the primary proper score matched to the label encoding, secondary metrics, the resampling unit, the paired tests, the exact meaning of "beats") is committed **before** the analysis script runs. Real multi-annotator ratings; a prompt/item-level train, validation and test split with no rater of an item in two splits; the unbiased per-sample proper risk at test time (exact with one rating per held-out item); item-clustered bootstrap 95% intervals (at least 1,000 draws); Holm-Bonferroni across the declared dataset-budget cells; failures and null results reported. "Beats" means a lower mean held-out proper score and a significant pre-registered paired test; a lower point estimate alone is never "beats". Synthetic laws only verify a proof against a known target and are labelled that way. The bar is a top machine-learning conference.
